2026-09-20T16:18:23.671976Z [] {'source_reference_id': 'DFAT-1111', 'source_id': 'austender', 'title': 'Mid-Term Review of the Climate Resilient Communities Program', 'issuing_agency': 'Department of Foreign Affairs and Trade', 'category': 'Request for Tender', 'status': 'open', 'publish_date': '2026-08-25', 'closing_date': '2026-09-21', 'value_amount': None, 'value_currency': 'AUD', 'value_notes': "Indicative budget of approximately AUD 300,000 plus Australian GST for the full scope of services. Austender also states 'From to $330,000.00'.", 'location': 'Indo-Pacific, Pacific, Southeast Asia, South Asia', 'description': "The Department of Foreign Affairs and Trade (DFAT) is seeking an independent evaluation team to conduct a Mid-Term Review (MTR) of its Climate Resilient Communities (CRC) facility. The CRC facility, valued at $234.9 million over five years (2024?29), currently supports 24 co-funded programs totaling $418 million. The review will assess the CRC's progress, performance, effectiveness, efficiency, governance, and value for money, including its contribution to climate resilience programming, climate integration within DFAT, and the performance of the CRC Support Unit. The scope of work involves review planning, data analysis, stakeholder consultations, surveys, field-based data collection, presentations, and the delivery of draft and final evaluation reports.\n\nThe successful team will be responsible for an Evaluation Plan, Aide Memoire, Draft Evaluation Report, and Final Evaluation Report. The MTR is expected to include two fieldwork visits of up to one week each, one in the Pacific and one in Southeast Asia/South Asia, for which tenderers should include reasonable costs. DFAT will provide relevant program documentation but no facilities, logistical support, security, or local-language/interpreter support. The review team should comprise 2-4 members with expertise in Climate Resilience, Gender Equality, Disability and Social Inclusion (GEDSI), Program Evaluation, and Capacity Building, though DFAT is open to flexible team structures.\n\nThis Request for Tender (DFAT-1111) was published on 25 August 2026 and closes on 21 September 2026 at 11:00 am ACT Local Time. The indicative budget for the full scope of services is approximately AUD 300,000 plus GST. The contract term is 6 months, from October 2026 to April 2027, with an option for a 3-month extension until July 2027. The evaluation process is expected to conclude with the final report by March 2027.\n\nOrganisations that are members of the CRC MEL Panel or have previously provided MEL technical advice are eligible but must address any perceived or actual conflicts of interest in their tender. The RFT does not require Australian Government security clearances for team members. The three evaluation criteria are of equal importance. The deadline for questions regarding this ATM passed on 14 September 2026.", 'summary_headline': 'DFAT seeks an independent evaluation team for the Mid-Term Review of the Climate Resilient Communities Program.', 'contact_name': 'Tender Secretariat, Climate Resilience and Finance Branch', 'contact_email': 'crcmtrenquiries@dfat.gov.au', 'contact_phone': None, 'lodgment_address': 'www.tenders.gov.au', 'documents': [{'file_name': 'CCS - Approach to Market for Evaluation Team for Mid-Term Review of the CRC Program.txt', 'extracted_text': "Approach to Market\nTo establish Contract for Evaluation Team - Mid-Term Review of the Climate Resilient Communities Program \nReference ID: DFAT-1111\nUNSPSC: 80101504 Strategic planning consultation services\nThis Approach to Market (ATM) is for the provision of an independent Mid-Term Review (MTR) of the Climate Resilient Communities (CRC) facility. \nThe Department of Foreign Affairs and Trade (DFAT) is seeking a suitably qualified evaluation team to assess CRC?s progress, performance, effectiveness, efficiency, governance arrangements and value for money midway through implementation. \nThe review will examine CRC?s contribution to climate resilience programming, climate integration across the development program, and capability strengthening within DFAT, as well as the performance of the CRC Support Unit and broader delivery arrangements. \nServices sought include review planning and design, document and data analysis, stakeholder consultations, survey design and implementation, field-based data collection, preparation of an aide memoire, presentations to stakeholders, and delivery of draft and final evaluation reports containing evidence-based findings and recommendations to inform the remainder of Phase 1 implementation and potential Phase 2 activation.\nThe Commonwealth of Australia as represented by Department of Foreign Affairs and Trade (the Customer) is seeking submissions for the provision of the services (the Requirement) as described in this ATM comprising:\nthis CCS ATM, including the Statement of Requirement\nthe CCS ATM Response Form\nthe CCS ATM Annexes (if any)\nthe Commonwealth ATM Terms\nthe Additional Contract Terms (if any)\nthe Commonwealth Contract Terms\nthe Commonwealth Contracting Suite Glossary and Interpretation.\nIn submitting a response, Potential Suppliers are required to comply with all requirements set out in the Commonwealth Approach to Market Terms (a copy of which is included in this document), and if successful, agree to enter into a contract which incorporates the Commonwealth Contract Terms available at https://www.finance.gov.au/government/procurement/commonwealth-contracting-suite-ccs#ccs-terms.\nThe Selling to Government website provides information to help businesses understand how to sell to the Australian Government. If you have any general questions about how the Australian Government procures goods and services, please contact the Selling to Government team at\u202fsellingtogov@finance.gov.au.\xa0\nFor questions relating specifically to this ATM, please contact the ATM Contact Officer set out at A.A.5(a).\xa0\nStatement of Requirement\nA.A.1\tKey Information and Dates\nApproach to Market\nProposed Contract\nA.A.2\tThe Requirement\nThe Department of Foreign Affairs and Trade (DFAT) requires the services of an independent review team to undertake a Mid-Term Review (MTR) of the Climate Resilient Communities (CRC) investment. The purpose of the review is to provide DFAT with an evidence-based assessment of CRC's performance, effectiveness, efficiency, governance arrangements, value for money and progress towards achieving intended outcomes, and to provide practical recommendations to inform implementation for the remainder of Phase 1 and any potential activation of Phase 2.\nThe successful supplier will be required to design and deliver an independent evaluation in accordance with DFAT's evaluation policies, standards and ethical requirements. Services are expected to include review planning and methodology development; document review and analysis; stakeholder consultation and engagement; design and implementation of data collection activities, including interviews, surveys and fieldwork; analysis and synthesis of qualitative and quantitative data; preparation and presentation of findings; and production of all required deliverables.\nThe review is expected to assess CRC's contribution to climate resilience programming, climate integration across the development program, capability strengthening within DFAT, governance and delivery arrangements, risk management, and performance of the Climate Resilient Communities Support Unit. The supplier will be required to provide independent findings and recommendations that support DFAT's strategic decision-making and continuous improvement objectives.\nThe successful supplier will be responsible for delivering, at a minimum, an Evaluation Plan, an Aide Memoire and stakeholder presentation, a Draft Evaluation Report, a Final Evaluation Report, and associated presentations and briefings. The supplier will be expected to engage with DFAT personnel and other stakeholders, undertake field visits in selected partner countries, and provide all personnel, management, expertise and resources necessary to complete the requirement.\nA.A.2(a)\tCommonwealth Supplier Code of Conduct\nThe Commonwealth expects its suppliers to conduct themselves with high standards of ethics such that they consistently act with integrity and accountability.\xa0\xa0\nIf awarded a Contract, the Supplier must agree to comply with the Commonwealth Code of Conduct (Code) in accordance with the Commonwealth Contract Terms [Clause C.C.23].\xa0\nClause C.C.23 requires the Supplier to comply with the Code when performing its obligations under the Contract, and to ensure its personnel and Subcontractors comply with the Code. The clause also requires the Supplier to proactively monitor and assess compliance with the Code, and to notify the Customer immediately of any breach of the Code.\xa0\xa0\nIn accordance with the Commonwealth ATM Terms [Clause A.B.10], where requested by the Customer, Potential Suppliers should provide information demonstrating that they have appropriate policies, frameworks, or similar, in place to comply with the Code.\xa0\xa0\nFurther information on the Code can be found at: https://www.finance.gov.au/government/procurement/commonwealth-supplier-code-conduct \nA.A.2(b)\tStandards\nPotential Suppliers must ensure that any goods and services proposed are capable of complying with all applicable Australian standards and any Australian and international standards specified in this Statement of Requirement. Potential Suppliers should note that they may be required to enable the Customer, or an independent assessor, to conduct periodic audits to confirm compliance with those standards.\nWeb Content Accessibility\nThe Supplier must ensure that any website, associated material and/or online publications (where applicable) complies with the Web Content Accessibility Guidelines available at: https://www.w3.org/WAI/intro/wcag.\nPublications\nPublications and reports (if any) must be drafted to comply with the current version of the Australian Government Style Manual available at: https://www.stylemanual.gov.au/.\nAustralian Standards\nThe Supplier must comply with the following Australian Standard(s):\nKey Performance Indicators\nThe Supplier is required to comply with the following Key Performance Indicators, sustainability targets or other performance measures to demonstrate progress towards achievement of the Requirement:\nThe performance indicators relevant to this engagement will be determined in consultation with DFAT and finalised during contract negotiation, with the agreed indicators to be incorporated into the timeline and work plans upon completion.\nA.A.2(c)\tSecurity Requirements\nNone Specified\nA.A.2(d)\tWork Health and Safety\nPrior to commencement of the Contract, the Customer?s Contract Manager and the Supplier?s Contract Manager will identify any potential work health and safety (WHS) issues anticipated to arise during the term of the contract and assign management of each issue identified to the party best able to manage it. For all issues assigned to the Supplier, the\xa0Supplier will provide the Customer with a WHS plan for approval and no work will commence until the plan is approved unless agreed in writing by the Customer.\nThroughout the Contract Term, the Customer and the Supplier will proactively identify and cooperate to manage any WHS issues that arise.\nA.A.2(e)\tDelivery and Acceptance\nThe Customer must accept or reject any deliverables under the Contract in accordance with the Commonwealth Contract Terms [Clause C.C.11].\nA.A.2(f)\tMeetings\nThe Supplier will be required to attend meetings as follows:\nA.A.2(g)\tFacilities and Assistance Offered by the Customer\nThe Customer will not make any facilities or assistance available to the Supplier.\nA.A.2(h)\tCustomer Material\nDFAT will make available to the Mid-Term Review (MTR) team information, documents and relevant materials relating to Climate Resilient Communities (CRC). These will include, but not be limited to, the documents outlined below. DFAT will make available to the Review team any other reasonable requests for information and documentation relating to the MTR. The Review team is also expected to independently source other relevant material and literature.\nClimate Resilient Communities Investment Design Document (INN793)\nReporting from the Climate Resilient Communities Support Unit (CRCSU) \nAnnual Reports and Quarterly Reports\nDFAT quality and performance reporting, including Investment Monitoring Reports (IMRs) and relevant reports from the QTAG\nDocumentation and reports from Steering Committee meetings\nProgram documentation, including facility and program-level governance materials, MEL Plan and performance assessment framework, meeting agendas and minutes, risk registers, and environmental and social safeguarding documentation, budgets.\nApproval, agreement and contracting documents (those not subject to commercial confidence), including documents relevant to the establishment of the facility, Phase 1 implementation arrangements, and the CRCSU contract\nDFAT Design, Monitoring and Evaluation Standards\nA.A.3\tATM Distribution\nAusTender Distribution\nThis ATM and any updates are subject to the AusTender\xa0Terms\xa0of\xa0Use. Any updates to this ATM will be distributed via AusTender. \nAusTender is the Australian Government?s procurement information system. Access to and use of AusTender is subject to the AusTender Terms of Use. In participating in this ATM process, Potential Suppliers must comply with the AusTender Terms of Use and any applicable instructions, processes, procedures and recommendations as advised on AusTender.\nA.A.4\tLodgement of Responses\nAusTender\nResponses must be lodged electronically via AusTender before the Closing Time and in accordance with the Response lodgement procedures set out in this ATM documentation and on AusTender.\nAll queries for technical or operational support must be directed to the AusTender Help Desk. Contact details are available on the Contact Us page of the AusTender website.\nThe Closing Time will be displayed on the relevant AusTender webpage together with a countdown clock that displays, in real time, the time left until Closing Time (for more information refer to AusTender\xa0Terms\xa0of\xa0Use).\nFor the purpose of determining whether a Response has been lodged before the Closing Time, the\xa0countdown clock will be conclusive.\nResponse File Format, Naming Convention and Size\nThe Customer will accept Responses lodged in the following formats:\nMicrosoft Word (.docx)\nPDF (.pdf)\nThe Response file name/s should:\nincorporate the Potential Supplier?s full legal organisation name; and \nreflect the various parts of the bid they represent (where the Response comprises multiple files).\nResponse files must not exceed a combined file size of 25 megabytes per upload.\nResponses must be completely self-contained. No embedded files can be included. No hyperlinked or other material may be incorporated by reference.\nA.A.5\tCustomer?s Contact Officers\nA.A.5(a)\tATM Contact Officer\nFor all matters relating to this ATM, the Contact Officer is:\nName/Position: Tender Secretariat, Climate Resilience and Finance Branch\nEmail Address:  crcmtrenquiries@dfat.gov.au\nNote: Question Closing Date and Time is set out at item A.A.1 [Key Information and Dates].\nA.A.5(b)\tComplaints Handling\nComplaints relating to this ATM should be directed to:\nInformation relating to the handling of complaints is available on the Customer?s website at: .\nIf your issue is not resolved refer https://www.finance.gov.au/business/procurement/complaints-handling-charter-complaints for more information relating to complaints.\nAdditional Contract Terms\nAn executed contract will incorporate the Commonwealth Contract Terms and also the following Additional Contract Terms:\nA.C.1\tIntellectual Property\nThe Customer owns the Intellectual Property Rights in the Material created under the Contract. \nTo the extent the Supplier or a third party holds any Intellectual Property Rights in any existing Material, the Supplier hereby agrees to licence the Customer to enable the Customer to exercise full rights and interests in the Intellectual Property Rights in any Material provided under the Contract. The Supplier agrees to create, execute or sign any documents and perform all acts which may be necessary to allow the use of those rights by the Customer for any purpose.\nThe Customer grants to the Supplier a non-exclusive, non-transferable, irrevocable, royalty-free licence for the Contract Term to exercise the Intellectual Property Rights in the Material for the sole purpose of fulfilling its obligations under the Contract. The licence in this clause is subject to any conditions or limitations of third parties that the Customer notifies to the Supplier.\nIntellectual Property Rights in Goods provided under the Contract or pre-existing Intellectual Property of the Supplier, set out below (if any), will not change as a result of the Contract.\nA.C.2\tPayment Terms\nThe Customer must pay the amount of a Correctly Rendered Invoice to the Supplier within thirty\xa0(30)\xa0calendar days after receiving it, or if this day is not a Business Day, on the next Business Day.\nA.C.3\tFraud\nA.C.3.1\tThis clause replaces clause C.C.22.F [Fraud] of the Commonwealth Contract Terms. For the purposes of this clause, ?Fraud? means, in delivering the Goods and/or Services under the Contract, dishonestly obtaining a benefit, or causing a loss, by deception or other means, and includes suspected, alleged or attempted fraud.\nA.C.3.2\tThe Supplier must take all reasonable steps to prevent and detect Fraud in relation to the performance of this Contract. \nA.C.3.3\tSubject to A.C.3.4, if the Supplier becomes aware of any Fraud, it must report the matter to the Customer in writing within five (5) business days.\nA.C.3.4\tIf the Supplier is under a legal obligation not to report a Fraud to the Customer, but local police or other law enforcement agencies provide an exception to permit reporting, the Supplier must report a Fraud to the Customer within five (5) business days of the exception being granted.\nA.C.3.5\tThe Supplier must investigate all Fraud at the Supplier?s expense and in accordance with any reasonable directions or standards required by the Customer. After the investigation is finished, the Supplier must promptly report full details of any Fraud to:\nthe Customer (unless the Supplier is under a legal obligation not to report a Fraud to the Customer); and\nthe local police and any other appropriate law enforcement agency in the country where the incident occurred, unless the Customer agrees otherwise in writing. \nA.C.3.6\tIf the investigation finds Fraud by the Supplier or its officers, employees or agents, or the Supplier has failed to take reasonable steps to prevent Fraud by a subcontractor, the Supplier must, if directed by the Customer, promptly reimburse or compensate the Customer in full.\nA.C.3.7\tThis clause is a material term of the Contract and survives the termination or expiry of the Contract.\nA.C.4\tProhibited dealings\nA.C.4.1\tThe Supplier must ensure that it and its officers, employees, agents and subcontractors involved in delivering Goods and or Services under this Contract are not:\ndirectly or indirectly engaged in preparing, planning, assisting or fostering a terrorist act;\nlisted terrorist organisations for the purposes of the\xa0Criminal Code Act 1995\xa0(Cth) (details of listed terrorist organisations are available at:\nhttps://www.nationalsecurity.gov.au/Listedterroristorganisations/Pages/default.aspx);\nsubject to sanctions or similar measures under the\xa0Charter of the United Nations Act 1945\xa0(Cth) or the\xa0Autonomous Sanctions Act 2011\xa0(Cth) (details of individuals and entities are available at:\nhttps://dfat.gov.au/international-relations/security/sanctions/Pages/consolidated-list.aspx;\nlisted on the ?World Bank?s Listing of Ineligible Firms and Individuals? posted at:\xa0http://www.worldbank.org/en/projects-operations/procurement/debarred-firms;\nowned, controlled by, acting on behalf of, or at the direction of persons, or entities referred to in clauses A.C.4.1(a) to A.C.4.1(d) above; or\nproviding direct or indirect support, resources or assets (including any grant monies) to persons or entities referred to in clauses A.C.4.1(a) to A.C.4.1(e) above.\nA.C.4.2\tWhere the Supplier becomes aware that there are reasonable grounds to suspect it or any of its officers, employees, agents and subcontractors has or may have contravened any part of clause A.C.4, the Supplier must:\nnotify the Customer and confirm that information in writing as soon as possible, which must be no later than within 24 hours;\nimmediately take all reasonable action to mitigate the risks; and\ntake any other action reasonably required by the Customer.\nA.C.4.3\tThe Supplier must ensure that any subcontract entered into by the Supplier for the purposes of fulfilling its obligations under this Contract imposes on the Subcontractor the same obligations that the Supplier has under this clause\xa0A.C.4.\nA.C.4.4\tThis clause is a material term of the Contract and survives the termination or expiry of the Contract.\nA.C.5\tAnti-corruption\nA.C.5.1\xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0 In this clause, ?Corrupt Conduct? means corrupt conduct as defined in the National Anti-Corruption Commission Act 2022 (Cth) or conduct that would satisfy that definition if any party involved in the conduct were a ?public official? as defined in that Act.\nA.C.5.2\xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0 The Supplier warrants that, to the best of its knowledge, it has disclosed to DFAT:\n(a) \tall allegations of, or investigations in relation to, Corrupt Conduct involving it, or its officers, employees, agents and subcontractors, that may substantially impact on compliance with Commonwealth policy and legislation or the Commonwealth?s reputation; and\n(b) \tany substantiated instances of Corrupt Conduct in the past [3] years, involving it, or its officers, employees, agents and subcontractors, that may substantially impact on compliance with Commonwealth policy and legislation or the Commonwealth?s reputation. \nA.C.5.3\xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0 The Supplier will not, and will ensure that its officers, employees, agents and subcontractors do not:\n(a) \tengage in Corrupt Conduct; \n(b) \tengage in any practice that, if engaged in within Australia, could constitute the offence of bribing a foreign public official under s 70.2 of the Criminal Code Act 1995 (Cth) in relation to the performance of the Contract; or \n(c) \xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0 engage in any other conduct which could actually or potentially compromise the integrity, accountability or probity of the administration of, or delivery of, this Contract. The Supplier will also not engage in any practice that could constitute the offence of failing to prevent bribery of a foreign public official under s 70.5A of the Criminal Code Act 1995 (Cth) in relation to the performance of the Contract. \nA.C.5.4\xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0 The Supplier acknowledges that it is responsible for preventing and detecting Corrupt Conduct.\nA.C.5.5\xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0 The Supplier will comply with any reasonable request, policy or direction issued by DFAT, and cooperate with DFAT, at its own cost, in relation to any action taken by DFAT required or authorised by the National Anti-Corruption Commission Act 2022 (Cth).\nA.C.5.6\xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0 Within three months of the Execution of this Deed, the Supplier will prepare a corruption risk assessment and corruption control strategy for the delivery of the Goods and Services and, if requested by DFAT, provide DFAT with the corruption control strategy within 10 business days of the request. The risk assessment and strategy will contain appropriate corruption prevention, detection, investigation and reporting processes and procedures that comply with the Commonwealth?s Fraud and Corruption Control Framework (http://www.ag.gov.au). The Supplier?s strategies will include:\n(a) \xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0 preparation and implementation of a corruption control strategy, policy and relevant procedures applicable to the Supplier, and its Personnel; \n(b) \xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0 development of guidance on corruption and delivery of materials and training to the Supplier?s Personnel; \n(c) \xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0 provision of mandatory corruption control awareness training to all of the Supplier?s Personnel and implementation of procedures to track attendance; and \n(d) any other matter requested by DFAT. \nA.C.5.7\xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0 If the Supplier knows or suspects any practice to have occurred, as described in Clause A.C.5.3, then it will:\n(a) \treport the matter and provide all relevant information to DFAT within five (5) business days; \n(b) \twithin 10 business days develop a strategy to investigate the relevant practice, for DFAT?s approval, based on the principles set out in the Australian Government Investigations Standards (https://www.ag.gov.au/sites/default/files/2022-12/Australian-Government-Investigations-Standard-2022.pdf); \n(c) \xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0 undertake the investigation in accordance with the approved strategy at the Supplier?s own cost; and \n(d) \treport to DFAT on the progress and outcome of the investigation. \nA.C.5.8\xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0 In addition to the investigation carried out by the Supplier under Clause A.C.5.7 above, DFAT or its nominee may conduct its own investigation. If DFAT exercises its rights under this Clause A.C.5, the Supplier will provide all reasonable assistance and information as requested at the Supplier?s expense.\nA.C.5.9\xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0 Following the conclusion of any investigation in connection with the performance of this Contract that finds that the Supplier or its officers, employees, agents and subcontractors, or a third party, have engaged in any practice, as described in Clause A.C.5.3 in connection with this Contract, the Supplier will, at its own cost:\n(a) \tmake every effort to recover any relevant Funds or Assets acquired or distributed through the relevant practice in accordance with recovery procedures, including civil litigation, available; \n(b) \trefer the matter to the police or other authorities responsible for prosecution of the relevant practice, unless an exemption has been sought and granted by the relevant DFAT delegate; \n(c) \tkeep DFAT informed, in writing, on a monthly basis, of the progress of the investigation and recovery action; and \n(d) \xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0 reimburse or return to DFAT any Funds or Assets misappropriated through the relevant practice that are recovered by the Supplier. \nA.C.5.10\xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0 If the Supplier considers that after all reasonable action has been taken under Clause A.C.5.9 to recover the Funds or Assets and full recovery has not been achieved or recovery has only been achieved in part, the Supplier:\n(a) \tmay seek approval from DFAT that no further recovery action be taken; and \n(b) \xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0 will provide to DFAT all information, records and documents required by DFAT to enable DFAT to make a decision on whether or not to approve non-recovery of losses arising from the relevant practice, or misappropriated Funds or Assets. \nA.C.5.11\xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0 The Supplier will ensure that any subcontract entered into for the purpose of this Contract contains a substantially equivalent clause granting the rights specified in this Clause A.C.5.\nA.C.6\tChild Protection\nA.C.6.1\tThe Supplier must comply, and must ensure that its officers, employees, agents and subcontractors comply with the Department of Foreign Affairs and Trade?s Child Protection Policy, accessible at http://www.dfat.gov.au/childprotection/.\nA.C.6.2\tThe Customer may conduct a review of the Supplier?s compliance with the Child Protection Policy. The Customer will give reasonable notice to the Supplier and the Supplier must participate co-operatively in any such review.\nA.C.6.3\tIf the Customer finds that the Supplier has failed to comply with the Child Protection Policy, the Supplier must promptly, and at the cost of the Supplier, take such actions as are required to ensure compliance with the Child Protection Policy.\nA.C.6.4\tIf an individual, the Supplier must sign and return to the Customer the Child Protection Professional Behaviours at Attachment B to the Child Protection Policy.\nA.C.6.5\tThis clause is a material term of the Contract and survives the termination or expiry of the Contract.\nA.C.7  \tProtection from Sexual Exploitation, Abuse and Harassment (PSEAH)\nA.C.7.1\xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0 In providing the Goods or Services to DFAT, the Contractor must comply with, and ensure that its Personnel, contractors and subcontractors comply with, DFAT?s Protection from Sexual Exploitation, Abuse and Harassment Policy, accessible at: http://www.dfat.gov.au/pseah.\nA.C.7.2\xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0 In accordance with DFAT?s Protection from Sexual Exploitation, Abuse and Harassment Policy, the Contractor must immediately (within 24 hours) report any suspected or alleged case of sexual exploitation, abuse and harassment by anyone within scope of the policy in connection with official duties or business to seah.reports@dfat.gov.au.\nA.C.7.3\xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0 In reporting to DFAT as required pursuant to clause A.C.7.2, the Contractor must comply with the Privacy Act 1988 (Cth), and the privacy provisions in the DFAT Incident Notification Form, accessible at http://www.dfat.gov.au/pseah.\nA.C.7.4\xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0 DFAT may conduct a review of the Contractor's compliance with DFAT's Protection from Sexual Exploitation, Abuse and Harassment Policy. DFAT will give reasonable notice (at least 14 calendar days) to the Contractor and the Contractor must at its own cost participate co-operatively in any such review.\nA.C.7.5\xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0 If DFAT finds that the Contractor has failed to comply with DFAT?s Protection from Sexual Exploitation, Abuse and Harassment Policy, the Contractor must promptly, and at the cost of the Contractor, take such actions as are required to ensure compliance with DFAT?s Protection from Sexual Exploitation, Abuse and Harassment Policy.\nA.C.7.6\xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0 This clause A.C.7 is a material term of the Contract.\nINSTRUCTIONS FOR POTENTIAL SUPPLIER\nBefore you start\nThis Response is not an offer for work.\nRead the all documentation provided by the Customer and distributed with this Response form and decide whether your organisation has the necessary skills and experience to meet the Customer?s requirement.\nDo not proceed further if:\nyour organisation cannot agree to the Commonwealth Contract Terms, available at https://www.finance.gov.au/government/procurement/commonwealth-contracting-suite-ccs. These terms are non-negotiable and will form part of the Contract if you are successful in this ATM process.\nyour organisation does not meet and/or agree to the Minimum Content and Format Requirements and the Conditions for Participation (if any), set out at item A.A.1 [Key Information and Dates], as failure to do so will mean your response cannot be considered.\nyour organisation is a trust where the Trustee is not empowered to sign contracts on behalf of the Trust. Before proceeding, contact the Customer?s ATM Contact Officer at item\xa0A.A.5(a) [ATM Contact Officer].\nyou are an Individual without an ABN and you do not meet the Australian Taxation Office?s (ATO) definition of an independent contractor available at: https://www.ato.gov.au/business/employee-or-contractor/how-to-work-it-out--employee-or-contractor. Before proceeding, contact the ATM Contact Officer set out at item\xa0A.A.5(a) [ATM Contact Officer] to seek advice.\nThe Customer will evaluate all valid Responses received by the ATM Closing Time [Item A.A.1] that meet the Minimum Content and Format Requirements and the Conditions for Participation (if any), to determine which Potential Supplier has proposed the best value for money outcome for the Customer. Responses will be evaluated as per the criteria set out at Clause A.B.5 [Evaluation].\nParticipation in this ATM is at your organisation?s risk and cost. Please note this is a competitive process and your organisation may incur costs in responding. If you are unsuccessful, you will be unable to recoup these costs.\nFormat Requirements\nYou MUST use this form (CCS ATM Response Form) to submit your Response, which MUST comply with the Commonwealth ATM Terms, available at: https://www.finance.gov.au/government/procurement/commonwealth-contracting-suite-ccs.\nAs this form is set out to facilitate the evaluation of responses, Potential Suppliers are to use the form as provided and are not permitted to make any changes to the structure or formatting of the document.\nGuidance for completing your Response\nSpecific questions about this ATM must be directed to the ATM Contact Officer set out at Item\xa0A.A.5(a) [ATM Contact Officer] before Question Closing Date and Time set out at item A.A.1 [Key Information and Dates]. \nIn preparation of this Response, please note the Commonwealth Indigenous Procurement Policy (IPP) available at: https://www.niaa.gov.au/indigenous-affairs/economic-development/indigenous-procurement-policy-ipp may apply to the Customer in respect of this procurement. During evaluation of responses, the Customer may consider the Supplier?s ability to assist the Customer to meet its IPP obligations.\nEnsure your Response is as concise as possible while including all information that your organisation wants the evaluation team to consider. Do not include general marketing material or assume that the evaluation team has any knowledge of your organisation?s capabilities or personnel.\nDo not include pricing in any other part of your response except in the relevant Pricing Schedule.\nThe successful Supplier will have demonstrated its ability to provide the best value for the Customer. This will not necessarily be the lowest price.\nSubmit the form as required by Item A.A.4 [Lodgement of Responses].\nIf your organisation is unsuccessful with this Response, you may request a debrief to assist with future responses. The ATM Contact Officer set out at Item A.A.5(a) [ATM Contact Officer] can arrange this for you.\nPotential Supplier?s Contact Officer\nFor all matters relating to this Response, the Potential Supplier?s Contact Officer is:\nPart 1 ? Potential Supplier?s Details\n1.1\tPotential Supplier?s Details\n1.2\tPrevious Judicial Decisions\n1.3\tWorkplace Gender Equality\n1.4\tIndigenous Businesses\n1.5\tAustralian Businesses\n1.6\tNew Zealand Businesses\n1.7\tPotential Supplier?s Contract Manager\nFor matters relating to the proposed Contract, the Potential Supplier?s Contract Manager will be:\n1.8\tPotential Supplier?s Address for Notices (if different from the Contract Manager)\nPart 2 ? Executive Summary\n2.1\tExecutive Summary of Potential Supplier?s Proposal\n2.2\tConditions for Participation\nPart 3 ? Ability to Meet the Requirement\n3.1\tDetailed Proposal to Meet the Customer?s Requirement\nCommonwealth Supplier Code of Conduct\nStandards\nPart 4 ? Potential Supplier?s Demonstrated Capability and Capacity\n4.1\tStatement of Skills and Experience\n4.2\tSpecified Personnel\n# if requested at A.A.2(b)\n4.3\tSubcontractors\nScope of Works to be Subcontracted\n4.4\tConflicts of Interest\nThe Potential Supplier has identified the following potential Conflicts of Interest and management strategies:\n4.5\tReferees\n4.6\tPre-existing Intellectual Property of Potential Supplier\n4.7\tConfidentiality of Potential Supplier?s Information\nThe Potential Supplier has assessed that the commercial information in the table below meets the requirements available at https://www.finance.gov.au/government/procurement/buying-australian-government/additional-reporting-confidentiality and is seeking that information be kept confidential.\n4.8\tProven Ability to Meet Regulatory Considerations\n4.9\tSustainability Considerations\n4.10\tAdditional Information\nPart 5 ? Total Costs to be incurred by the Customer\n5.1\tPricing\nFixed Price (including all expenses)\nAdjustment to Fixed Pricing for Contract Variation/Extension\n5.2\tProposed Payment Schedule\n5.3\tAdditional Facilities and Assistance\n5.4\tNon-Compliance\n"}, {'file_name': 'DFAT-1111 - Addendum 1.txt', 'extracted_text': ' \n1 \n \nOFFICIAL \nOFFICIAL \n \n \nADDENDA 1 \nto the Request for Tender DFAT-1111 ? Mid-Term Review of the Climate Resilient Communities \nProgram released on 25 August 2026. \nAll other information as set out in the RFT dated 25 August 2026 remains unchanged. \nDFAT provides the following information in accordance with the Standard Conditions of Tender.  \nQuestions and Answers: \nRef \nQuestion \nAnswer \n1 \nCould DFAT please clarify whether \norganisations that are members of the CRC \nMEL Panel and/or have previously provided \nMEL technical advice to the CRC Support \nUnit are eligible to submit a tender for the \nMid-Term Review procurement? \nThe RFT is an open Request for Tender and does \nnot identify the CRC MEL Panel, or previous \nprovision of MEL technical advice to the CRC \nSupport Unit, as a specific exclusion from \nparticipation. Organisations that are members of \nthe CRC MEL Panel and/or have previously \nprovided MEL technical advice to the CRC Support \nUnit are therefore eligible to submit a tender. \nTenderers are encouraged to address in their \ntender response how they would manage any \nperceived or actual conflict of interest under Part \n4.4 of the Response to Approach the Market Form. \nDFAT will consider any proposed conflict of interest \nmanagement approach as part of its assessment. \n \n2 \nAre there weighted evaluation criteria for \nthe assessment of tender responses? If so, \ncould DFAT please provide details of the \nevaluation criteria and any associated \nweightings? \nThe three criteria set out in the CCS ATM Terms \nand Conditions (A.B.5.3) are of equal importance. \n3 \nCould DFAT please provide the indicative \nbudget range, expected contract value, \nupper funding limit, or anticipated budget \nfor this procurement to assist suppliers in \ndeveloping proposals that are \nproportionate to the required scope, \nmethodology, fieldwork and deliverables? \nFor indicative planning purposes, tenderers may \nassume an indicative budget of approximately AUD \n300,000 plus Australian GST as required, for the \nfull scope of services. Tenderers should ensure \ntheir proposed methodology, team composition, \nfieldwork and deliverables are proportionate to the \nRFT requirements and represent value for money. \n4 \nCould DFAT please provide a list of the 24 \nCRC sub-programs, including their locations \nand, if available, the value of each \ninvestment, to assist suppliers in \nunderstanding the program portfolio, \npotential field visit requirements, and \nappropriate team composition? \nSpecific information on CRC sub-programs, \nincluding locations and investment values, is \nsensitive and may only be provided to the \nsuccessful supplier, subject to relevance, \nconfidentiality and procurement requirements. For \ninformation on potential field visit requirements \nand team composition, please refer to the \nresponse to Question 5. \n \n5 \nCould DFAT please provide further \nguidance on the expected fieldwork \nrequirements, including: indicative \nDFAT anticipates that the Mid-Term Review will \ninclude two fieldwork visits of up to one week \neach. Tenderers should not assume travel to \n\n \n2 \n \nOFFICIAL \nOFFICIAL \ncountries to be visited in the Pacific and \nSouth Asia/Southeast Asia regions; \nexpected duration of field visits; and \nwhether suppliers should assume travel to \nmultiple countries within each region? \nmultiple countries within each region. At this stage, \nDFAT envisages one fieldwork visit in the Pacific \nand one fieldwork visit in Southeast Asia; however, \nthese locations are not confirmed and may be \nsubject to change. Tenderers may propose a team \nstructure that includes participation by a subset of \nthe evaluation team in fieldwork activities, where \nthis represents an efficient and appropriate \napproach to delivering the review. \n \n6 \nHas DFAT commissioned, received, or relied \nupon any previous evaluations, reviews, \nassessments, MEL products, or other \nadvisory work relating to the Climate \nResilient Communities Program that may \nbe relevant to this Mid-Term Review? \nDFAT has an independent Quality Technical \nAdvisory Group that provides reviews and \nassessments relevant to CRC implementation. CRC \nis also subject to standard DFAT investment \nmonitoring, reporting and evaluation \nrequirements, including routine investment \nmonitoring reports and general program reporting. \nAny documentation that would assist the review \nteam will be made available to the successful \ntenderer, subject to relevance, confidentiality and \nprocurement requirements. \n7 \nIs DFAT willing to extend the tender closing \ndate, including by up to one week, to allow \nsuppliers additional time to prepare \nresponses? \nDFAT does not intend to extend the tender closing \ndate.  \n8 \nDoes implementing a CRC co-funded \nprogram conflict a supplier out of \nconducting this MTR? We are implementing \nsteps to mitigate any perceived conflict, \nand will explain the COI management \napproach in our response. We would like to \nconfirm if this is sufficient. \nImplementing a CRC co-funded program does not \nautomatically exclude a supplier from applying to \nconduct the Mid-Term Review. Tenderers are \nencouraged to address in their tender response \nhow they would manage any perceived or actual \nconflict of interest under Part 4.4 of the Response \nto Approach the Market Form. DFAT will consider \nany proposed conflict of interest management \napproach as part of its assessment. \n \n9 \nFrom our review of the ATM documents, \nthere is a page limit articulated for the \nexecutive summary, but not for other \nsections. Please confirm if this means there \nis no page limit for other sections of the \nresponse. \nThere is no page limit for the other sections of the \nresponse, but we encourage responses to be kept \nreadable and succinct. \n \n---End of Addendum No. 1-- \n \n\n'}, {'file_name': 'DFAT-1111 - Addendum 2 - Attachment 1 - Updated ToR fo CRC Mid-Term Review.txt', 'extracted_text': "TERMS OF REFERENCE \nMID-TERM REVIEW OF THE CLIMATE RESILIENT \nCOMMUNITIES (CRC) (2024?29) \n1. BACKGROUND \nClimate Resilient Communities (CRC) is a facility, funded through the Department of Foreign \nAffairs and Trade (DFAT), with an initial value of $234.9 million and an initial investment \nterm of five years (2024?29), with the option to extend for a further four years. \n \nCRC is designed to be both a programming and an enabling facility that offers additional \nresources, specialist skills and capability across DFAT, including Posts, to help deliver \nAustralia?s commitment to climate action and finance to maximise impact and efficiency.  \n \nSpecifically, CRC operates to:  \n? help identify, design, co-fund and implement programs to strengthen climate \nresilience, particularly in the water, food and energy sectors, in line with partner \ncountry priorities  \n? offer administrative efficiencies, including support for the recruitment, deployment \nand management of technical assistance and the management of design and \nprocurement processes support the integration of climate resilience across the \ndevelopment program to meet Australia?s International Development Policy climate \nchange targets  \n? support monitoring, evaluation and learning (MEL); knowledge management; GEDSI; \nand strategic communications.  \n \nAs at mid-term, CRC is supporting 24 co-funded programs, contributing $149 (36%) million \nto a combined total investment value of $418 million. CRC uses a Contractor to manage a \nSupport Unit (CRCSU), up to $70 million for the first five years) to help DFAT deliver the \nfacility objectives. Some of the contracted personnel are? embedded in DFAT, who are \nprocured through the facility. Their placement in DFAT for specific roles builds DFAT?s \ncapability to deliver risk-informed climate resilience programming and communicate \nlearning. \nThe CRCSU has experienced an underspend during Phase 1 implementation, reflecting the \ntiming and sequencing of facility establishment, mobilisation and delivery. In response, \nfunding has been reprofiled to later years of Phase 1, creating an opportunity for the MTR to \nconsider whether current resourcing, delivery arrangements and contract options remain \n\nfit-for-purpose. This includes assessing how any unspent Support Unit funds could be used \nto support Phase 2, should that be approved. \n \n \n?Embedded? Personnel \nThe CRC embedded personnel model places specialist advisers and program support officers \nwithin DFAT to facilitate access to climate-related technical expertise, support management \nof the CRC including co-funded programs and help strengthen the Department?s long-term \ncapability to integrate climate considerations across the development program. Embedded \npersonnel work alongside DFAT staff on a day-to-day basis, providing additional capacity \nand expertise in areas such as climate resilience, disaster resilience, climate reporting, \nstrategic communications and program management. The model is designed to ensure \ntechnical advice is grounded in DFAT policies, priorities and operational realities, while \nsupporting Posts, desks and partners through technical guidance, quality assurance, \ncapability development, corporate reporting, risk management, knowledge sharing and \ncross-program learning. Embedded personnel are managed operationally by DFAT, with \nemployment and administrative support provided through the CRCSU. \n \nQuality and Technical Assurance Group (QTAG) \n \nDFAT manages co-funding for climate programs and appoints an independent Quality and \nTechnical Assurance Group (QTAG), which provides independent technical and quality \nassurance, in line with the Investment Design approved in 2023. The QTAG is responsible for \nundertaking annual independent assessments of CRC performance against the Performance \nAssessment Framework, alongside broader advisory support. \n  \nThe QTAG serves as a ?critical friend? to the program. It reviews workplans, governance \narrangements, MEL systems and reporting processes; contribute to learning and adaptation; \nand undertake targeted deep dives into specific workstreams, themes or partnerships.  \n \nSteering Committee  \nDFAT also appointed an internal, cross-Departmental Steering Committee to monitor \nprogram implementation, provide strategic direction and monitor risks. The Steering \nCommittee is supported by the Climate Integration team, which sets the agenda, prepares \nstanding agenda items (e.g., on risk), prepares minutes etc.. \n \nIn each geographic context, CRC works to amplify the voices of diverse women, persons with \ndisabilities and other marginalised groups and work with these people to identify context-\n\nappropriate outcomes that challenge inequality. Political economy and context assessments \nin partner countries have explicit attention to informal and formal power dynamics that \nexclude or marginalise diverse women, persons with disabilities and marginalised groups, \nparticularly in the context of water, food and energy. \n CRC focuses on the systemic barriers and reform needed to drive progress towards climate-\nresilient water, food and energy systems, to positively affect communities, businesses and \neconomies over the long term. It supports local solutions, fostering the local engagement \nand leadership needed to drive policy, regulatory and institutional reform. \nThe CRC directly supports Australia?s International Development Policy and aligns with: \n- \nAustralia?s International Gender Equality Strategy \n- \nAustralia?s International Disability Equity and Rights Strategy: Advancing equity to \ntransform lives \n- \nDFAT?s Climate Change Action Strategy \n \n1.1 CRC PROGRAM LOGIC \n \nClimate Resilient Communities is designed to deliver impact so that:  \n \nPeople in the Indo-Pacific are more resilient to climate change and disasters.  \n \nIt will contribute to this goal by achieving two End-of-Facility Outcomes (EoFOs):  \n1. Indo-Pacific partner governments have taken gender-responsive and inclusive \nactions that strengthen climate and disaster resilience, particularly in the water, food \nand energy sectors. \n2. Indo-Pacific development partners have implemented gender-responsive, inclusive \nand locally led programs and partnerships that strengthen climate and disaster \nresilience, particularly in the water, food and energy sectors. \n \nThese two mutually reinforcing outcomes recognise the critical place of the water, food and \nenergy sectors and the central role of gender equality, disability and social inclusion (GEDSI) \nin climate resilience. They will be pursued in tandem across Climate Resilient Communities \nactivities (refer to Annex 1 for Program Theory of Change). \n \nThe two EoFOs will be realised through achievement of the following Intermediate \nOutcomes (IOs):  \n \n1. Co-funded climate resilience programming and partnerships: DFAT?s Desks, Posts \nand implementing partners across the Indo-Pacific have engaged with local \n\nstakeholders to design and deliver gender responsive and inclusive climate resilience \nprograms, particularly in the water, food and energy sectors.  \n2. Integrated climate and disaster resilience across the development program: DFAT?s \nimplementing partners have integrated climate and disaster resilience in \ndevelopment programming across the Indo-Pacific in gender-responsive, inclusive, \nsector-relevant and locally appropriate ways. Implementing partners include but are \nnot limited to: Contractors, NGOs, universities, research organisations, government \nagencies, multilateral agencies and private sector entities. \n3. Enhanced DFAT capability to deliver climate and disaster-resilient development \nprograms with partner governments: DFAT?s climate and disaster resilience \ntechnical, advisory and programming capability has been enhanced and is supporting \npartner government and Australian Government climate resilience priorities in the \nIndo-Pacific.  \n \nNote: CRC?s Theory of Change is currently being revised. The Mid-Term Review will assess \nthe program against the revised Theory of Change, once finalised. The program logic above \nreflects the Theory of Change as it currently stands. \n \n2. REVIEW, PURPOSE, AND SCOPE OF THE MID-TERM REVIEW \nThe mid-term review (MTR) will assess CRC?s progress against the Investment?s \nEnd-of-Facility Outcomes, Intermediate Outcomes and workstreams. \nThe objectives of the MTR are to: \n1. Provide an evidence-based assessment of CRC implementation and progress to \ndate against the program outcomes, including the effectiveness of the facility in \nsupporting co-funded climate resilience programming, supporting climate \nintegration across the development program, and strengthening DFAT capability; \n2. Assess the appropriateness, quality and efficiency of the facility modality, including \ngovernance arrangements, management structures, decision-making processes and \nthe respective roles of DFAT, the embed structure, the Climate Resilient \nCommunities Support Unit (CRCSU), and other delivery partners; \n3. Review program expenditure, financial management and controls to assess \nwhether resources have been used economically and efficiently, and in line with \nDFAT?s value-for-money principles; \n4. Assess the performance of the CRCSU contract, including the effectiveness, \nefficiency and value add of the Support Unit model, and examine options for the \n\ncontinuation of the contract, including consideration of a no-cost extension and/or a \ncosted extension for the program; \n5. Assess the identification, management and mitigation of risks across the CRC, \nincluding whether risk management approaches remain appropriate as the program \nmatures and whether emerging risks are being effectively anticipated and \naddressed; \n6. Capture and synthesise key achievements, challenges and lessons from \nimplementation across co-funded programs, climate integration support, learning, \nand public diplomacy; and \n7. Provide forward-looking, practical recommendations to inform DFAT decision-\nmaking on possible changes for Phase 1, as well as activating Phase 2 of the \nprogram. This includes recommendations on adjustments required to the facility?s \ndesign, scope, resourcing or delivery arrangements to drive further impact and \nefficiency gains; and a recommendation on the size, scope and focus of any co-\nfunded programs \nThe MTR will seek to answer a set of Key Evaluation Questions (KEQ) identified in Section 4 \nbelow. These will be confirmed and refined as necessary at the start of the MTR as part of \nthe Evaluation Plan drafting process. \nThe MTR will take a holistic review of the program, including the managing contractor, \nimplementation team, existing advisory functions such as the Quality and Technical Advisory \nGroup (QTAG), governance structures, and DFAT management. \nThe MTR will also consider historical contexts such as budget changes, changes to Program \nLogic, and broader policy shifts to offer a comprehensive understanding of the Facility?s \nevolution and status. \n \n3. KEY AUDIENCES \nThe primary intended audiences for the MTR are: \n- \nDFAT Thematic Areas \no Climate Resilience and Finance Branch \no Disaster Risk Reduction and Preparedness \n- \nIndo ? Pacific Posts \n- \nDFAT Desks covering Indo-pacific Posts and Regional Climate programs  \n- \nDevelopment Policy Committee \n- \nMembers of the CRCSU Steering Committee \n- \nImplementing Partners \no DT Global - Climate Resilient Communities Support Unit (CRCSU) Contractor \n \n\n4. PROPOSED KEY EVALUATION QUESTIONS \nThe draft key evaluation questions (KEQ) and lines of enquiry, to be refined in collaboration \nwith the Review Team Leader during the planning phase, are as follows: \nKEQ 1: How effectively is CRC progressing towards its intended outcomes, and what \ncontribution is it making to Australia?s broader climate investment portfolio \n1.1. How effectively has CRC supported DFAT Posts and Desks to design, co-fund and deliver \nclimate resilience programming aligned with partner country priorities, including \nNationally Determined Contributions (NDCs) and National Adaptation Plans (NAPs)?  \n1.2. To what extent is CRC supporting greater climate integration and building the capability \nof DFAT staff to deliver investments with a climate objective, including by enhancing \nunderstanding of climate data, themes and challenges, to ensure climate investments \nmeet Australia?s International Development Policy climate change targets?  \n1.3 To what extent is CRC supporting integration of wider social inclusion, including First \nNations/ Indigenous perspectives, and intersectional GEDSI, in the implementation of \nclimate investments? \n1.4 To what extent is the CRC likely to achieve and sustain its outcomes by the end of the \ninvestment period? What are key drivers of success and what are key barriers?  \nKEQ 2: How efficiently have CRC resources been used to date, and how fit-for-purpose is \nthe facility and support unit model for the next phase of delivery? \n2.1 How efficiently have CRC resources been allocated and utilised across co-funded \nprograms, technical assistance, learning, MEL, GEDSI and public diplomacy? \n2.2 How efficient has the Climate Resilient Communities Support Unit (CRCSU) been in \ndelivering its contracted functions, including program cycle support, technical \nassistance, embedded personnel and facility-level services for DFAT Posts, Desks and \npartners? \n2.3 How effective and appropriate are CRC's risk management and safeguarding systems in \nidentifying, mitigating and responding to existing and emerging risks, and what changes, \nif any, will be required to manage risks and safeguard implementation during the \nremainder of Phase 1 and any potential Phase 2? \n \nKEQ 3: To what extent are the governance and implementation arrangements serving \neffective delivery of CRC Outcomes?  \n\n3.1 To what extent are the governance and delivery arrangements appropriate, including \nthe Steering Committee, Secretariat, CRCSU and DFAT Posts and Desks? What \napproaches have been effective and what could be improved? \n3.2 How clear, effective and proportionate are the management and delivery arrangements \nat the facility and program levels, including roles and responsibilities across DFAT, the \nCRCSU and other partners? \nKEQ 4: What are the key lessons from the MTR and recommendations to strengthen CRC \nin the remainder of Phase 1 and a possible Phase 2?  \n4.1 What changes are required to CRC?s resourcing, and CRCSU contract model to address \nbudget pressures, efficiency and value for money during the remainder of Phase 1 and \nto support DFAT decision-making on activating Phase 2 of the CRC program including \nwhether a no-cost or costed extension is appropriate to absorb contract underspend? \n4.2 How could the monitoring and reporting of CRC achievements and contributions to the \nclimate investment portfolio (including their visibility) be improved? What adjustments, \nif any, are recommended to refine monitoring, evaluation and learning frameworks? \n \n5. METHODOLOGY \nThe MTR will be conducted in line with DFAT policies (M&E Standards and Ethical Research \nand Evaluation Guidance). The MTR methodology and approach will be refined by the \nReview team in agreement with DFAT. \nIt is expected the review will include: \n? Desk review of relevant documentation provided by DFAT. \n? Key Informant Interviews (KII) and an online survey with internal and external \nstakeholders (including DFAT Posts, CRCSU, DFAT Contracts and Procurement Team) \n? Two geographic/field visits to observe implementation and gather firsthand insights \n(1 visit in the Pacific region, 1 visit in the Southeast Asia/South Asia region) \n? Data analysis and synthesis of findings into an MTR report with clear \nrecommendations for activating Phase 2 and contract options for CRCSU. \n? Sampling approaches to data collection, which should be explained and justified in \nthe evaluation plan. \n \n6. PROCESS \n1. DFAT will provide a verbal briefing of the key issues and priority information before \ninitial document review and preparation of a plan for the review.  \n2. DFAT will collate and share program documentation with the Review Team.  \n\n3. The Review Team will undertake an initial document review and develop a detailed \nReview Plan including any proposed revisions to the KEQs. The Review Plan will be \nsubmitted as draft at least four weeks before mobilisation to the field and must \nmeet Standard 9 of the DFAT Design and Monitoring, Evaluation and Learning \nStandards.  \n4. The Review Team will undertake remote data collection prior to conducting field \nvisits. Scheduling will allow for adequate data collection and analysis to answer key \nreview questions.  \n5. The Review Team will provide updates to the nominated DFAT representatives to \nmonitor progress, report on the emergence of any issues of concern and provide \nfeedback. The schedule of these update meetings is to revolve around key outputs \nand milestones, with specific dates to be agreed with DFAT at a later date. \n6. Before completing the draft MTR report, the Review Team will conduct a feedback \nsession with select DFAT staff and present an aide memoire summarising key \nfindings and recommendations.  \n7. A draft report will include findings presented in formats to aid understanding and \ncommunication. The report should include quantitative and qualitative data sources \nsystematically synthesised into findings and reflect a clear, transparent process for \nmaking judgments against KEQs. The report must meet Standard 10 of the the DFAT \nDesign and Monitoring, Evaluation and Learning Standards.   \n8. Stakeholders, including DFAT, will review the draft report and ensure the process for \nconsultation and commenting is efficient and allows independence of \nthe review team?s final report.   \n \n7. EXPECTED OUTPUTS AND TIMEFRAMES  \nThe evaluation process is expected to commence by September 2026 and conclude with the \nfinal report to be submitted to DFAT by March 2027. The process is expected to include \nboth desk-based activities and in-country missions. \nKey Activities \nIndicative due \ndate \nDescription  \nVerbal briefing by \nDFAT \n15 October 2026 \nDFAT provides verbal briefing to MTR Team \nand access to key documents \nDraft evaluation plan \nand document \nreview by MTR Team \n1 November 2026 \nDevelopment of an evaluation plan that \nmeets DFAT M&E Standard 9 ? including key \nevaluation questions with detailed \ndescription, data sources against each \nquestion/sub-question, methods to collect \nand analyse data, a detailed timeline, a list of \nkey evaluation informants, proposed \nschedule for in-country work, preliminary \ndata collection instruments, a detailed \n\nbreakdown of responsibilities of all team \nmembers and estimated costings. The plan \nwill be developed in close consultation with \nDFAT.  \nFinal Evaluation Plan \nby MTR Team \n(indicative payment \nmilestone) \n15 November \n2026 \nConsultation and finalisation of the Final \nEvaluation Plan which should address all \ncomments and questions on the draft \nprovided by DFAT and stakeholders and \ninclude the booking of key meetings. \nInformation \nGathering by MTR \nTeam \n15 November \n2026 ? 31 January \n2027 \nFurther document review and appraisal, in \ncountry data collection, fieldwork and \ninterviews, data analysis. \nAide Memoire and \nPresentation by MTR \nTeam (indicative \npayment milestone) \n1 February 2027 \nThe Aide Memoire (up to 5 pages) should \npresent the initial findings, seek verification \nof facts and assumptions, and discuss the \nfeasibility of initial recommendations. \nDevelopment of a summary PowerPoint and \npresentation to the Steering Committee on \ninitial findings, seeking feedback. The \naudience for this document will be key \nstakeholders. \nDraft report by MTR \nTeam \n15 March 2027 \nThe Draft Evaluation Report (up to 25 pages \nexcluding 2-page executive summary and \nannexes) should address the evaluation \nquestions and be targeted to the needs of \nintended users. The report should have a \nsuccinct and clear executive summary of \nfindings (2 pages) and a summary of the \nrecommendations (1 page); written in plain \nEnglish that can be read as a stand-alone \ndocument. Key achievements and challenges \nshould be clearly presented in the executive \nsummary, throughout the report and should \nbe evidence-based. The conclusions and \nrecommendations should be practical and \nstrategic; judgements should be clear and \nunambiguous, and reasoning for the \njudgements should be transparent. Draft \nReport presentation to be developed and \npresented to the Steering Committee to seek \nfeedback. \nFinal report by MTR \nTeam (indicative \npayment milestone) \n15 April 2027 \nThe final report, summary of findings in the \nform of a final version of the PowerPoint \npresentation, incorporating any agreed \nchanges or amendments. The final evaluation \nreport must meet DFAT M&E Standard 10. \n\nManagement \nResponses \n30 April 2027 \nDFAT to consult on proposed management \nresponses \n \nThe process for commenting on deliverables will be streamlined to ensure efficiency while \nmaintaining the independence of the Evaluation Team?s Final MTR Report (approx. 20 pages \n+ annexes). Payment milestones are indicated in the table above but are indicative only and \nwill be confirmed and stipulated in the contract. \nAn indicative budget range for the MTR will be provided to guide bid development and \nensure proposals are proportionate to the expected scope, methodology, fieldwork \nrequirements and deliverables. The final budget will be confirmed through the procurement \nand contracting process. \nAll findings and recommendations will be based on evidence and aligned with DFAT?s \nDevelopment Evaluation Policy. The final report and DFAT management response will be \npublished on the DFAT website in line with the Development Evaluation Policy and \naccessibility requirements. DFAT will work with the MTR Team on framing any conclusions \nthat contain sensitive information. \n \n8. KEY DOCUMENTS \nDFAT will make available to the Mid-Term Review (MTR) team information, documents and \nrelevant materials relating to Climate Resilient Communities (CRC). These will include, but \nnot be limited to, the documents outlined below. DFAT will make available to the Review \nteam any other reasonable requests for information and documentation relating to the \nMTR. The Review team is also expected to independently source other relevant material and \nliterature. \n? Climate Resilient Communities Investment Design Document (INN793) \n? Reporting from the Climate Resilient Communities Support Unit (CRCSU) \no Annual Reports and Quarterly Reports \n? DFAT quality and performance reporting, including Investment Monitoring Reports \n(IMRs) and relevant reports from the QTAG \n? Documentation and reports from Steering Committee meetings \n? Program documentation, including facility and program-level governance materials, \nMEL Plan and performance assessment framework, meeting agendas and minutes, \nrisk registers, and environmental and social safeguarding documentation, budgets. \n? Approval, agreement and contracting documents (those not subject to commercial \nconfidence), including documents relevant to the establishment of the facility, Phase \n1 implementation arrangements, and the CRCSU contract \n? DFAT Design, Monitoring and Evaluation Standards \n\n9. TEAM COMPOSITION AND GOVERNANCE \nThe Mid-Term Review (MTR) of Climate Resilient Communities (CRC) will be conducted by \nan independent review team comprising 2-4 members covering the following areas of \nexpertise: \n? Climate Resilience  \n? Gender Equality, Disability and Social Inclusion (GEDSI)  \n? Program Evaluation  \n? Capacity Building \nDFAT welcomes proposals for review teams that reflect its priorities of gender equality and \nlocalisation. \nThe review team will be contracted by DFAT and will report to DFAT for all contractual and \nadministrative matters under the Terms of Reference (ToR). \nAn MTR Steering Committee, comprising DFAT representatives (including the Climate \nResilience and Finance Branch and relevant geographic areas), will support the MTR by \nproviding oversight and coordinated feedback on findings through attendance at an online \npresentation of preliminary findings and recommendations. However, responsibility for final \nMTR conclusions is solely a matter for the MTR team. \nDFAT will coordinate consolidated, prioritised feedback from the Steering Committee to the \nreview team. \nThe MTR team will be responsible for the technical quality, independence and integrity of \nthe review, and for the preparation of all deliverables, including the Review Plan, Aide \nMemoire, Draft Report and Final Report. \n10. ETHICAL CONSIDERATIONS  \n \nThe Evaluation Team will adhere to the highest ethical standards to ensure integrity and \ncredibility in conducting this review.  \nThe Evaluation Team will ensure that informed consent is obtained from all participants and \nthat information is treated confidentially.  \nThe Evaluation Team will strictly comply with the Australasian Evaluation Society (AES) Code \nof Ethics and the DFAT Aid Evaluation Policy.  \nTo safeguard participants' rights and uphold transparency, informed consent will be obtained \nin writing from all review participants where feasible. They will be clearly informed about the \nnature of the information being collected, the methods of recording, and its intended use. \n\nWhere this is not feasible, the evaluation team will ensure all participants are informed of the \npurpose of the MTR and their freedom to opt out of all or part of the process, without \nprejudice. \n\nAnnexure 1 ? Program Theory of Change \n \n \n \n\nAnnexure 2 ? Funding Flows \n \n\n"}, {'file_name': 'DFAT-1111 - Addendum 2.txt', 'extracted_text': ' \n1 \n \nOFFICIAL \nOFFICIAL \n \n \nADDENDA 2 \nto the Request for Tender DFAT-1111 ? Mid-Term Review of the Climate Resilient Communities \nProgram released on 25 August 2026. \nAll other information as set out in the RFT dated 25 August 2026 remains unchanged. \nDFAT provides the following information in accordance with the Standard Conditions of Tender.  \nAttachments: \n1. Updated Terms of Reference for CRC Mid-Term Review (amendment to key evaluation \nquestions, see question and answer 14) \nQuestions and Answers: \nRef \nQuestion \nAnswer \n1 \nCould DFAT please clarify whether \norganisations that are members of the CRC \nMEL Panel and/or have previously undertaken \nCRC-related MEL or technical advisory tasks, \nincluding for the CRC Support Unit, DFAT \nprograms or SPREP, are eligible to submit a \ntender for the Mid-Term Review \nprocurement? \nThis has been answered previously and included \nin Addendum 1.  \n2 \nCould DFAT please provide the indicative \nbudget range or budget envelope for this \nproject? \nThis has been answered previously and included \nin Addendum 1.  \n3 \nAre you planning to host an information \nsession in relation to the RFT? \nDFAT does not intend to hold an information \nsession for this RFT at this stage. Any \nclarifications will be provided through formal \naddenda to ensure all potential suppliers receive \nthe same information. \n4 \nAre selection criteria available for the RFT, and \nare there weighted evaluation criteria or \nassociated weightings? \nThis has been answered previously and included \nin Addendum 1.  \n5 \nShould tenderers include the cost of the two \ngeographic/field visits in their proposal \npricing, and will priority countries, required \ncountries or pricing assumptions be provided \nif the fieldwork locations are not specified? \nThis has been answered previously and included \nin Addendum 1 in relation to indicative \nfieldwork assumptions. Tenderers should \ninclude reasonable costs for the two fieldwork \nvisits in their proposal pricing. DFAT anticipates \ntwo fieldwork visits of up to one week each, \ncomprising one visit in the Pacific and one visit \nin Southeast Asia or South Asia. Tenderers \nshould not assume travel to multiple countries \nwithin each region. Specific locations are not \nconfirmed and may be subject to change. \n\n \n2 \n \nOFFICIAL \nOFFICIAL \n6 \nCan DFAT confirm the correct briefing date, \nproposed contract commencement date, and \nfull milestone schedule, including the dates \nfor the Evaluation Plan, Draft Evaluation \nReport, Final Evaluation Report and \npresentation? \nThe dates in the RFT documentation are \nindicative and will be finalised in the contract \nwith the successful supplier. For the purposes of \npreparing a tender response to this RFT, \ntenderers should refer to the dates set out in \nthe Terms of Reference. \n7 \nCan DFAT provide more information on the 24 \nco-funded programs, including jurisdiction, \nactivity, funding recipients, co-funders, \ncountry or region, investment value and \nimplementation phase, and approximate \nstakeholder population size? \nThis has been answered previously and included \nin Addendum 1.  \n8 \nAre details of the membership of the CRC \nSupport Unit and Steering Committee \navailable? \nDetails of CRC Support Unit and Steering \nCommittee membership may be provided to the \nsuccessful supplier where relevant to delivery of \nthe Mid-Term Review, subject to confidentiality \nand procurement requirements. \n9 \nIs any CRC progress documentation, including \ndocumentation on baseline data, the CRC MEL \nPlan, Performance Assessment Framework, \nIMRs, QTAG assessments, CRCSU reporting or \nother evidence sources publicly available to \nassist suppliers in preparing a tender \nresponse? \nThis has been answered previously and included \nin Addendum 1 in relation to relevant CRC \ndocumentation. \n10 \nCan DFAT clarify whether section 3.1(a) \nrequires tenderers to address the \nCommonwealth Supplier Code of Conduct \nbeyond completing the tick box, including \nwhether additional information on policies, \nframeworks or similar is required? \nTenderers should complete the relevant \nrequirement in accordance with the RFT \ndocumentation. Unless otherwise specified in \nthe RFT, tenderers are not required to provide \nadditional supporting information beyond the \nrequested response. Tenderers remain \nresponsible for ensuring they are compliant with \nall applicable RFT requirements. \n11 \nAre supplier team members required to hold \nAustralian Government security clearances? \nThe RFT does not require supplier team \nmembers to hold Australian Government \nsecurity clearances. \n12 \nWhat are the intended page limits for the \ndraft and final Mid-Term Review reports? \nTenderers should refer to the RFT \ndocumentation for any specified page limits. \nWhere no page limit is specified, tenderers \nshould ensure deliverables are fit for purpose, \nanalytical, concise and proportionate to the \nscope of the Mid-Term Review. \n13 \nWhich program logic should tenderers use in \ntheir bid: the three Intermediate Outcomes in \nthe body of the ToR, the four Intermediate \nOutcomes in Annex 1, or the pending revised \nTheory of Change? \nTenderers should use the program logic \nincluded in the RFT documentation for the \npurposes of preparing their tender response. \nDFAT will provide any updated Theory of \nChange or additional program logic \ndocumentation to the successful supplier where \nrelevant to delivery of the Mid-Term Review. \n14 \nIs the duplication of Key Evaluation Questions \n2.3 and 4.2 deliberate? \nThe duplication of Key Evaluation Questions 2.3 \nand 4.2 was not deliberate. DFAT will issue a \ncorrection to the RFT documentation removing \nKey Evaluation Question 4.2. \nSee Attachment 1 to this Addendum \n\n \n3 \n \nOFFICIAL \nOFFICIAL \n15 \nGiven in-country field visits are required, can \nDFAT clarify whether any planning or logistics \nassistance will be available to the review team \non the ground, including Post-facilitated \nintroductions, security or travel advisories, or \nlocal-language/interpreter support? \nTenderers should prepare proposals on the \nbasis of the RFT documentation. DFAT may \nfacilitate reasonable engagement with relevant \nstakeholders where appropriate and subject to \noperational requirements, but tenderers should \nnot assume dedicated facilities, logistical \nsupport, security or local-language/interpreter \nsupport will be provided unless expressly stated \nin the RFT documentation. \n16 \nGiven the specified areas of expertise, would \nDFAT accept a proposal with a single evaluator \nwho is qualified and experienced across all \nrequired areas? \nTenderers may propose a team composition \nthey consider best suited to deliver the Mid-\nTerm Review. Tenderers should ensure their \nproposed team demonstrates the required \nexpertise, independence, capacity and ability to \ndeliver the full scope of services within the \nrequired timeframe. DFAT will assess the \nproposed team composition as part of the \ntender evaluation process. \n \n---End of Addendum No. 1-- \n \n\n'}, {'file_name': 'DFAT-1111 -- Addendum 3.txt', 'extracted_text': '1 \nOFFICIAL \nOFFICIAL \nADDENDA 3 \nto the Request for Tender DFAT-1111 ? Mid-Term Review of the Climate Resilient Communities \nProgram released on 25 August 2026. \nAll other information as set out in the RFT dated 25 August 2026 remains unchanged. \nDFAT provides the following information in accordance with the Standard Conditions of Tender. \nThe deadline for questions passed on 14 September 2026 at 5:00pm ACT local time.  DFAT will not \nprovide  responses to questions asked after this time. \nQuestions and Answers: \nRef \nQuestion \nProposed response \n1 \nSection 9 of the Terms of Reference \nspecifies a team of 2?4 members covering \nclimate resilience, GEDSI, program \nevaluation and capacity building. Is DFAT \nopen to individual team members \ncovering more than one area of \nexpertise? \nTenderers may propose a team \ncomposition they consider best suited to \ndeliver the Mid-Term Review, which may be \nmore than the indicated 2 ? 4 member \nrecommendation. Individual team members \nmay cover more than one area of expertise, \nprovided the tenderer demonstrates that \nthe proposed team collectively has the \nrequired expertise, independence, capacity \nand ability to deliver the full scope of \nservices within the required timeframe. \nThis will contribute to DFAT?s assessment of \ntenderers? capability and response to \ndeliver the Requirement. \n2 \nWould locally engaged evaluators be \ncounted within the 2?4 member team \nspecified in Section 9, or in addition to it? \nLocally engaged evaluators may be included \nwithin the proposed 2?4-member team \nwhere they form part of the tenderer?s core \nproposed team. Tenderers may also \nidentify additional locally engaged \npersonnel or advisers where relevant to \ndelivery. Tenderers should clearly describe \neach person?s role, level of effort and \ncontribution to the review methodology. \n3 \nGiven the range of expertise sought, \nwould DFAT consider a team larger than \nfour personnel, including a team of five \npersonnel or additional experts providing \ntargeted technical inputs? \nTenderers may propose a team \ncomposition they consider best suited to \ndeliver the Mid-Term Review, including \nadditional targeted technical inputs where \nthey consider these necessary and \nproportionate. Tenderers should ensure the \nproposed team structure remains efficient, \nproportionate to the scope and indicative \n\n2 \nOFFICIAL \nOFFICIAL \nbudget, and demonstrates value for money. \nThis will contribute to DFAT?s assessment of \ntenderers? capability and response to \ndeliver the Requirement.  \n4 \nCould DFAT confirm whether it is \nacceptable to include a project \nmanagement role or resource within the \nproposed team, noting this is not listed as \none of the identified areas of expertise in \nSection 9? \nTenderers may include project \nmanagement capability within their \nproposed approach where they consider \nthis necessary for effective delivery of the \nMid-Term Review. Tenderers should clearly \nidentify whether project management is \nprovided by a team member, an additional \nresource, or through the tenderer?s \norganisational support arrangements, and \nensure the approach is proportionate and \nrepresents value for money. \n5 \nCould DFAT indicate the circumstances in \nwhich the three-month extension option \nwould be exercised and how tenderers \nshould price it in the proposal if \ntriggered? \nThe extension option may be exercised at \nDFAT?s discretion, subject to operational \nrequirements, satisfactory performance, \ncontinued need, and any relevant internal \napprovals. Tenderers should price their \nresponse in accordance with the RFT \ndocumentation and clearly state any \nassumptions about the extension option, \nincluding any rates or pricing basis that \nwould apply if the option were exercised. \n6 \nCould DFAT specify the entities the \nsuccessful supplier would need to speak \nwith in each country, including Southeast \nAsian countries such as Indonesia, \nVietnam, the Philippines, Thailand and \nSingapore? \nThis has been answered previously and \nincluded in earlier addenda in relation to \nindicative fieldwork assumptions and \nstakeholder engagement. \n7 \nThe table at A.A.2(e) sets the Draft \nEvaluation Plan as due 22 October 2026, \nwhile the Proposed Start Date at A.A.1 is \n25 October 2026. The dates at A.A.2(e) \nalso differ from those in Section 7 of the \nTerms of Reference by about a month for \nthe Draft and Final Reports. Which dates \nshould tenderers price and plan against, \nand will the schedule be adjusted at \ncontract signing? \nThis has been answered previously and \nincluded in earlier addenda in relation to \nmilestone dates.  \n8 \nWhat level of financial and contract \ndocumentation will the successful \ntenderer receive, including CRCSU \nexpenditure and underspend data and the \nCRCSU contract itself? \nDFAT may provide relevant financial, \nexpenditure and contract documentation to \nthe successful supplier where necessary for \ndelivery of the Mid-Term Review, subject to \nconfidentiality, privacy, commercial \nconfidence and any other applicable \nrequirements. Tenderers should prepare \ntheir proposals on the basis of the \ninformation included in the RFT \ndocumentation. \n\n3 \nOFFICIAL \nOFFICIAL \n9 \nCan DFAT give an indicative number of \nkey informants and an indicative size of \nthe survey population, including the \nnumber of embedded personnel within \nDFAT? \nTenderers should prepare proposals on the \nbasis of the RFT documentation and \npropose a consultation approach that is \nproportionate, targeted and fit for purpose. \nDFAT may provide further stakeholder \ninformation to the successful supplier \nwhere relevant to delivery of the Mid-Term \nReview, subject to confidentiality and \noperational requirements. \n10 \nPart 5.1 requires a fixed price covering all \ntravel. Addendum 1 states that fieldwork \nlocations are not confirmed and may \nchange. Will DFAT accept a fixed price \nbased on a stated travel assumption, with \nthe price adjusted under Part 5.1 if the \nconfirmed destinations differ? \nThis has been answered previously and \nincluded in earlier addenda in relation to \nindicative fieldwork assumptions.  \n---End of Addendum No. 3-- \n\n'}, {'file_name': 'DFAT-1111.txt', 'extracted_text': '================================================================================\nAUSTENDER DETAILS: Current ATM View - DFAT-1111\n================================================================================\n\nDetail URL: https://www.tenders.gov.au/Atm/Show/ae889192-19d8-4f96-a4bc-f3c1d067adf0\nScraped At: 2026-09-20 16:16:32\n\nSTRUCTURED ATTRIBUTES:\n----------------------------------------\nATM ID: DFAT-1111\nAgency: Department of Foreign Affairs and Trade - Australian Aid Program\nCategory: 80101504 - Strategic planning consultation services\nClose Date & Time: 21-Sep-2026 11:00 am (ACT Local Time) Show close time for other time zones\nPublish Date: 25-Aug-2026\nLocation: ACT, NSW, VIC, SA, WA, QLD, NT, TAS Canberra, Sydney, Melbourne, Adelaide, Perth, Brisbane, Darwin, Hobart\nATM Type: Request for Tender\nMulti Agency Access: No\nPanel Arrangement: No\nMulti-stage: No\nDescription: The Department of Foreign Affairs and Trade (DFAT) is seeking a suitably qualified evaluation team to assess CRC?s progress, performance, effectiveness, efficiency, governance arrangements and value for money midway through implementation. The review will examine CRC?s contribution to climate resilience programming, climate integration across the development program, and capability strengthening within DFAT, as well as the performance of the CRC Support Unit and broader delivery arrangements. Services sought include review planning and design, document and data analysis, stakeholder consultations, survey design and implementation, field-based data collection, preparation of an aide memoire, presentations to stakeholders, and delivery of draft and final evaluation reports containing evidence-based findings and recommendations to inform the remainder of Phase 1 implementation and potential Phase 2 activation.\nOther Instructions: \nConditions for Participation: Please reference the attached ATM documents for full Conditions of Participation, and Minimum Content and Format Requirements\nTimeframe for Delivery: 6 months (October 2026 to April 2027) with an option to extend for 3 months (May 2027 to July 2027)\nEstimated Value (AUD): From to $330,000.00\nAddress for Lodgement: www.tenders.gov.au\nAddenda Available: View Addenda\n\n================================================================================\n'}, {'file_name': 'ToR for CRC Mid-Term Review - Final.txt', 'extracted_text': "TERMS OF REFERENCE \nMID-TERM REVIEW OF THE CLIMATE RESILIENT \nCOMMUNITIES (CRC) (2024?29) \n1. BACKGROUND \nClimate Resilient Communities (CRC) is a facility, funded through the Department of Foreign \nAffairs and Trade (DFAT), with an initial value of $234.9 million and an initial investment \nterm of five years (2024?29), with the option to extend for a further four years. \n \nCRC is designed to be both a programming and an enabling facility that offers additional \nresources, specialist skills and capability across DFAT, including Posts, to help deliver \nAustralia?s commitment to climate action and finance to maximise impact and efficiency.  \n \nSpecifically, CRC operates to:  \n? help identify, design, co-fund and implement programs to strengthen climate \nresilience, particularly in the water, food and energy sectors, in line with partner \ncountry priorities  \n? offer administrative efficiencies, including support for the recruitment, deployment \nand management of technical assistance and the management of design and \nprocurement processes support the integration of climate resilience across the \ndevelopment program to meet Australia?s International Development Policy climate \nchange targets  \n? support monitoring, evaluation and learning (MEL); knowledge management; GEDSI; \nand strategic communications.  \n \nAs at mid-term, CRC is supporting 24 co-funded programs, contributing $149 (36%) million \nto a combined total investment value of $418 million. CRC uses a Contractor to manage a \nSupport Unit (CRCSU), up to $70 million for the first five years) to help DFAT deliver the \nfacility objectives. Some of the contracted personnel are? embedded in DFAT, who are \nprocured through the facility. Their placement in DFAT for specific roles builds DFAT?s \ncapability to deliver risk-informed climate resilience programming and communicate \nlearning. \nThe CRCSU has experienced an underspend during Phase 1 implementation, reflecting the \ntiming and sequencing of facility establishment, mobilisation and delivery. In response, \nfunding has been reprofiled to later years of Phase 1, creating an opportunity for the MTR to \nconsider whether current resourcing, delivery arrangements and contract options remain \n\nfit-for-purpose. This includes assessing how any unspent Support Unit funds could be used \nto support Phase 2, should that be approved. \n \n \n?Embedded? Personnel \nThe CRC embedded personnel model places specialist advisers and program support officers \nwithin DFAT to facilitate access to climate-related technical expertise, support management \nof the CRC including co-funded programs and help strengthen the Department?s long-term \ncapability to integrate climate considerations across the development program. Embedded \npersonnel work alongside DFAT staff on a day-to-day basis, providing additional capacity \nand expertise in areas such as climate resilience, disaster resilience, climate reporting, \nstrategic communications and program management. The model is designed to ensure \ntechnical advice is grounded in DFAT policies, priorities and operational realities, while \nsupporting Posts, desks and partners through technical guidance, quality assurance, \ncapability development, corporate reporting, risk management, knowledge sharing and \ncross-program learning. Embedded personnel are managed operationally by DFAT, with \nemployment and administrative support provided through the CRCSU. \n \nQuality and Technical Assurance Group (QTAG) \n \nDFAT manages co-funding for climate programs and appoints an independent Quality and \nTechnical Assurance Group (QTAG), which provides independent technical and quality \nassurance, in line with the Investment Design approved in 2023. The QTAG is responsible for \nundertaking annual independent assessments of CRC performance against the Performance \nAssessment Framework, alongside broader advisory support. \n  \nThe QTAG serves as a ?critical friend? to the program. It reviews workplans, governance \narrangements, MEL systems and reporting processes; contribute to learning and adaptation; \nand undertake targeted deep dives into specific workstreams, themes or partnerships.  \n \nSteering Committee  \nDFAT also appointed an internal, cross-Departmental Steering Committee to monitor \nprogram implementation, provide strategic direction and monitor risks. The Steering \nCommittee is supported by the Climate Integration team, which sets the agenda, prepares \nstanding agenda items (e.g., on risk), prepares minutes etc.. \n \nIn each geographic context, CRC works to amplify the voices of diverse women, persons with \ndisabilities and other marginalised groups and work with these people to identify context-\n\nappropriate outcomes that challenge inequality. Political economy and context assessments \nin partner countries have explicit attention to informal and formal power dynamics that \nexclude or marginalise diverse women, persons with disabilities and marginalised groups, \nparticularly in the context of water, food and energy. \n CRC focuses on the systemic barriers and reform needed to drive progress towards climate-\nresilient water, food and energy systems, to positively affect communities, businesses and \neconomies over the long term. It supports local solutions, fostering the local engagement \nand leadership needed to drive policy, regulatory and institutional reform. \nThe CRC directly supports Australia?s International Development Policy and aligns with: \n- \nAustralia?s International Gender Equality Strategy \n- \nAustralia?s International Disability Equity and Rights Strategy: Advancing equity to \ntransform lives \n- \nDFAT?s Climate Change Action Strategy \n \n1.1 CRC PROGRAM LOGIC \n \nClimate Resilient Communities is designed to deliver impact so that:  \n \nPeople in the Indo-Pacific are more resilient to climate change and disasters.  \n \nIt will contribute to this goal by achieving two End-of-Facility Outcomes (EoFOs):  \n1. Indo-Pacific partner governments have taken gender-responsive and inclusive \nactions that strengthen climate and disaster resilience, particularly in the water, food \nand energy sectors. \n2. Indo-Pacific development partners have implemented gender-responsive, inclusive \nand locally led programs and partnerships that strengthen climate and disaster \nresilience, particularly in the water, food and energy sectors. \n \nThese two mutually reinforcing outcomes recognise the critical place of the water, food and \nenergy sectors and the central role of gender equality, disability and social inclusion (GEDSI) \nin climate resilience. They will be pursued in tandem across Climate Resilient Communities \nactivities (refer to Annex 1 for Program Theory of Change). \n \nThe two EoFOs will be realised through achievement of the following Intermediate \nOutcomes (IOs):  \n \n1. Co-funded climate resilience programming and partnerships: DFAT?s Desks, Posts \nand implementing partners across the Indo-Pacific have engaged with local \n\nstakeholders to design and deliver gender responsive and inclusive climate resilience \nprograms, particularly in the water, food and energy sectors.  \n2. Integrated climate and disaster resilience across the development program: DFAT?s \nimplementing partners have integrated climate and disaster resilience in \ndevelopment programming across the Indo-Pacific in gender-responsive, inclusive, \nsector-relevant and locally appropriate ways. Implementing partners include but are \nnot limited to: Contractors, NGOs, universities, research organisations, government \nagencies, multilateral agencies and private sector entities. \n3. Enhanced DFAT capability to deliver climate and disaster-resilient development \nprograms with partner governments: DFAT?s climate and disaster resilience \ntechnical, advisory and programming capability has been enhanced and is supporting \npartner government and Australian Government climate resilience priorities in the \nIndo-Pacific.  \n \nNote: CRC?s Theory of Change is currently being revised. The Mid-Term Review will assess \nthe program against the revised Theory of Change, once finalised. The program logic above \nreflects the Theory of Change as it currently stands. \n \n2. REVIEW, PURPOSE, AND SCOPE OF THE MID-TERM REVIEW \nThe mid-term review (MTR) will assess CRC?s progress against the Investment?s \nEnd-of-Facility Outcomes, Intermediate Outcomes and workstreams. \nThe objectives of the MTR are to: \n1. Provide an evidence-based assessment of CRC implementation and progress to \ndate against the program outcomes, including the effectiveness of the facility in \nsupporting co-funded climate resilience programming, supporting climate \nintegration across the development program, and strengthening DFAT capability; \n2. Assess the appropriateness, quality and efficiency of the facility modality, including \ngovernance arrangements, management structures, decision-making processes and \nthe respective roles of DFAT, the embed structure, the Climate Resilient \nCommunities Support Unit (CRCSU), and other delivery partners; \n3. Review program expenditure, financial management and controls to assess \nwhether resources have been used economically and efficiently, and in line with \nDFAT?s value-for-money principles; \n4. Assess the performance of the CRCSU contract, including the effectiveness, \nefficiency and value add of the Support Unit model, and examine options for the \n\ncontinuation of the contract, including consideration of a no-cost extension and/or a \ncosted extension for the program; \n5. Assess the identification, management and mitigation of risks across the CRC, \nincluding whether risk management approaches remain appropriate as the program \nmatures and whether emerging risks are being effectively anticipated and \naddressed; \n6. Capture and synthesise key achievements, challenges and lessons from \nimplementation across co-funded programs, climate integration support, learning, \nand public diplomacy; and \n7. Provide forward-looking, practical recommendations to inform DFAT decision-\nmaking on possible changes for Phase 1, as well as activating Phase 2 of the \nprogram. This includes recommendations on adjustments required to the facility?s \ndesign, scope, resourcing or delivery arrangements to drive further impact and \nefficiency gains; and a recommendation on the size, scope and focus of any co-\nfunded programs \nThe MTR will seek to answer a set of Key Evaluation Questions (KEQ) identified in Section 4 \nbelow. These will be confirmed and refined as necessary at the start of the MTR as part of \nthe Evaluation Plan drafting process. \nThe MTR will take a holistic review of the program, including the managing contractor, \nimplementation team, existing advisory functions such as the Quality and Technical Advisory \nGroup (QTAG), governance structures, and DFAT management. \nThe MTR will also consider historical contexts such as budget changes, changes to Program \nLogic, and broader policy shifts to offer a comprehensive understanding of the Facility?s \nevolution and status. \n \n3. KEY AUDIENCES \nThe primary intended audiences for the MTR are: \n- \nDFAT Thematic Areas \no Climate Resilience and Finance Branch \no Disaster Risk Reduction and Preparedness \n- \nIndo ? Pacific Posts \n- \nDFAT Desks covering Indo-pacific Posts and Regional Climate programs  \n- \nDevelopment Policy Committee \n- \nMembers of the CRCSU Steering Committee \n- \nImplementing Partners \no DT Global - Climate Resilient Communities Support Unit (CRCSU) Contractor \n \n\n4. PROPOSED KEY EVALUATION QUESTIONS \nThe draft key evaluation questions (KEQ) and lines of enquiry, to be refined in collaboration \nwith the Review Team Leader during the planning phase, are as follows: \nKEQ 1: How effectively is CRC progressing towards its intended outcomes, and what \ncontribution is it making to Australia?s broader climate investment portfolio \n1.1. How effectively has CRC supported DFAT Posts and Desks to design, co-fund and deliver \nclimate resilience programming aligned with partner country priorities, including \nNationally Determined Contributions (NDCs) and National Adaptation Plans (NAPs)?  \n1.2. To what extent is CRC supporting greater climate integration and building the capability \nof DFAT staff to deliver investments with a climate objective, including by enhancing \nunderstanding of climate data, themes and challenges, to ensure climate investments \nmeet Australia?s International Development Policy climate change targets?  \n1.3 To what extent is CRC supporting integration of wider social inclusion, including First \nNations/ Indigenous perspectives, and intersectional GEDSI, in the implementation of \nclimate investments? \n1.4 To what extent is the CRC likely to achieve and sustain its outcomes by the end of the \ninvestment period? What are key drivers of success and what are key barriers?  \nKEQ 2: How efficiently have CRC resources been used to date, and how fit-for-purpose is \nthe facility and support unit model for the next phase of delivery? \n2.1 How efficiently have CRC resources been allocated and utilised across co-funded \nprograms, technical assistance, learning, MEL, GEDSI and public diplomacy? \n2.2 How efficient has the Climate Resilient Communities Support Unit (CRCSU) been in \ndelivering its contracted functions, including program cycle support, technical \nassistance, embedded personnel and facility-level services for DFAT Posts, Desks and \npartners? \n2.3 How effective and appropriate are CRC's risk management and safeguarding systems in \nidentifying, mitigating and responding to existing and emerging risks, and what changes, \nif any, will be required to manage risks and safeguard implementation during the \nremainder of Phase 1 and any potential Phase 2? \n \nKEQ 3: To what extent are the governance and implementation arrangements serving \neffective delivery of CRC Outcomes?  \n\n3.1 To what extent are the governance and delivery arrangements appropriate, including \nthe Steering Committee, Secretariat, CRCSU and DFAT Posts and Desks? What \napproaches have been effective and what could be improved? \n3.2 How clear, effective and proportionate are the management and delivery arrangements \nat the facility and program levels, including roles and responsibilities across DFAT, the \nCRCSU and other partners? \nKEQ 4: What are the key lessons from the MTR and recommendations to strengthen CRC \nin the remainder of Phase 1 and a possible Phase 2?  \n4.1 What changes are required to CRC?s resourcing, and CRCSU contract model to address \nbudget pressures, efficiency and value for money during the remainder of Phase 1 and \nto support DFAT decision-making on activating Phase 2 of the CRC program including \nwhether a no-cost or costed extension is appropriate to absorb contract underspend? \n4.2 How effective and appropriate are CRC's risk management and safeguarding systems in \nidentifying, mitigating and responding to existing and emerging risks, and what changes, \nif any, will be required to manage risks and safeguard implementation during the \nremainder of Phase 1 and any potential Phase 2? \n4.3 How could the monitoring and reporting of CRC achievements and contributions to the \nclimate investment portfolio (including their visibility) be improved? What adjustments, \nif any, are recommended to refine monitoring, evaluation and learning frameworks? \n \n5. METHODOLOGY \nThe MTR will be conducted in line with DFAT policies (M&E Standards and Ethical Research \nand Evaluation Guidance). The MTR methodology and approach will be refined by the \nReview team in agreement with DFAT. \nIt is expected the review will include: \n? Desk review of relevant documentation provided by DFAT. \n? Key Informant Interviews (KII) and an online survey with internal and external \nstakeholders (including DFAT Posts, CRCSU, DFAT Contracts and Procurement Team) \n? Two geographic/field visits to observe implementation and gather firsthand insights \n(1 visit in the Pacific region, 1 visit in the Southeast Asia/South Asia region) \n? Data analysis and synthesis of findings into an MTR report with clear \nrecommendations for activating Phase 2 and contract options for CRCSU. \n? Sampling approaches to data collection, which should be explained and justified in \nthe evaluation plan. \n \n\n6. PROCESS \n1. DFAT will provide a verbal briefing of the key issues and priority information before \ninitial document review and preparation of a plan for the review.  \n2. DFAT will collate and share program documentation with the Review Team.  \n3. The Review Team will undertake an initial document review and de
